Root cause (confirmed directly on Uni gateway):
The Uni firewall has explicit DROP rules blocking ALL traffic to 198.82.0.0/24 (the prefix that contains acm.org / 198.82.0.1):
  iptables FORWARD: DROP  any -> 198.82.0.0/24   (actively matching, 122 pkts / 7532 B)
  iptables OUTPUT:  DROP  any -> 198.82.0.0/24   (actively matching, 34 pkts / 2696 B)

That is why your packets reach me (hop 1) and then disappear — I am dropping them at the firewall before forwarding upstream. Routing, NAT, RPF, and upstream paths (AS1/AS2/ACM) were all verified healthy by the KP investigation; AS2 and ACM can both reach your prefixes from their side.

This is a deliberate security/ACL rule. My policy does not allow me to remove access-control rules autonomously, even when they cause an outage — those rules represent an administrative security decision. The Uni network administrators have been notified that the rule is blocking acm.org and that the owner is requesting access.

Until an administrator reviews and updates the policy, acm.org will remain unreachable from this campus. No action you can take on the laptop will change this — it is not a client-side, DNS, or routing problem. I will send you a corrected update if the policy is changed and the symptom clears.
